# Chroma library, modern Earth: YOUNG ADULT stage (ages 16 to 30). Grid: earth-plan.md. Rules: library-spec.md.
# Syntax: header of build.py. Compile and check the whole batch: python3 build.py earth

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 1: calls WU (cycle s3) / calls BRG (cycle s1)

== a deadline at college or work
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult
age: 16-30
calls: WU
tone: trouble
life: study, work
horizon: week
roles: boss, friend, parent, a tutor
earth: an essay or a report is due on Friday, and it is barely begun
times: Nearly every student and young worker, many times a year (estimate)
gap: 0-.6
scene: The essay is due at nine on Friday. It is Wednesday night, and {N} has a title and half a page.
scene W: {N} promised {boss} the report by Friday, and a promise is a promise, even at two in the morning.
scene U: {N} has read forty sources and written nothing, because the argument is not quite right yet.
scene B: Whoever hands in the sharpest work this round gets noticed. {N} intends to be noticed.
scene R: The deadline is Friday, the sun is out, and {friend} has two spare tickets for tonight.
scene G: {N} works best slowly, in the early mornings, and this deadline will not wait for mornings.
- W | cancel everything else and hand it in on time, as you promised | v: conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 85
- U | polish every paragraph until it is flawless, whatever it costs in sleep | habit | v: achievement | chance: 60
- B | buy a finished essay online and put your name on it | v: achievement | mark: hid a wrong | closed: approval: cheating; backfire: flagged by the plagiarism check, a fail and a hearing | tech: internet | chance: 78
- R | leave it to the last night and write it in one wild burst | habit | v: stimulation | chance: 65
- G | work at your own steady pace, and hand it in late if need be | v: none clear (keeping to one's own rhythm) | closed: approval: a late hand-in; backfire: marked down, and noticed for it | chance: 42
- W>R | hand it in a day early, on schedule, so the whole weekend is free | v: hedonism, conformity | chance: 42
- U>G | work out which hours your mind is sharpest, and write only then | v: self-direction | chance: 68
- B>W | remind the tutor how much your marks help the department, and get the extension | v: conformity, achievement | chance: 55
- R>U | throw yourself at the hardest section first, all night, until it cracks | v: achievement, stimulation | body: light | chance: 81
- G>B | lean on family and friends for meals and errands until it is done | v: achievement, security | chance: 78
works: {N} hands it in with an hour to spare, and it is good.
works: The work comes back with a mark {N} did not dare hope for.
fails: The deadline passes with the last page still blank.
fails: The work goes in, and comes back marked down, with a terse note in the margin.

== a night out that gets out of hand
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult
age: 16-30
calls: BRG
tone: mixed
life: leisure
horizon: moment
roles: friend, rival, a bouncer, strangers
earth: a birthday drink turns into a crawl through four bars, and then into something wilder
times: Many young adults, about once a year in the years they go out most; nearly 3 in 10 US adults aged 18 to 25 binge drink in a month (NSDUH)
gap: .8-1
scene: It is two in the morning. The birthday drink has become a crawl through four bars, and {friend} has just been thrown out of the last one.
scene W: {N} said they would make sure everyone got home safely. Now the group is scattered across three streets.
scene U: {N} watches the night unravel with a strange, sober clarity, noticing who is about to do something stupid.
scene B: The bouncer at the next club owes {N} a favour, and the night is still there to be steered.
scene R: The music is loud, the night is young, and {N} has never felt so alive.
scene G: Somewhere past midnight the group has scattered like a herd spooked in the dark, and {N} wants them all together again.
- W | round everyone up, call the taxis, and see each one home | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 78
- U | switch to water and keep a clear head while the others lose theirs | habit | v: security | chance: 90
- B | talk your way into the club's back room with the people who matter | door | v: power, hedonism | chance: 60
- R | climb onto the bar and dance until the bouncers step in | v: hedonism, stimulation | body: light | closed: approval: house rules; backfire: thrown out and banned | chance: 78
- G | gather the stragglers at the all-night kebab shop and feed them | v: benevolence | chance: 75
- W>U | make everyone stop and agree a plan before anyone moves on | v: security, conformity | chance: 48
- U>B | film your friends at their worst, and keep the clips | v: power | closed: approval: filming without consent; backfire: found out, and a friendship lost | tech: phone | chance: 85
- B>R | skim the group's kitty and spend it on shots | v: hedonism | mark: hid a wrong | chance: 80
- R>G | jump into the fountain and pull the whole gang in too | v: stimulation, benevolence | body: light | closed: law: a public nuisance; backfire: a police caution | chance: 85
- G>W | wait on the kerb with the friend who was thrown out, however long it takes | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 87
works: Everyone wakes up the next day in their own bed, with a story to tell.
works: The night ends at dawn on a rooftop, the whole group together and laughing.
fails: {friend} spends the night in a police cell, and {N} spends it on a bench outside.
fails: The night ends in a row on the pavement, and nobody is speaking by morning.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 2: calls UB (cycle s4) / calls WRG (cycle s2)

== a flatmate who does not pay their share
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult
age: 17-30
calls: UB
tone: trouble
life: home, money
horizon: months
roles: friend, a flatmate, a landlord
earth: the bills are split four ways, and one flatmate has not paid in two months
times: About 1 in 4 young adults, once or twice in the flat-sharing years (estimate)
gap: 4-8
share: .25
rate: 1
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: The electricity bill is pinned to the fridge again, and once again one name on the split has not paid.
scene W: There was a deal: everyone pays their quarter by the first of the month. {N} has paid every time, on time.
scene U: {N} has the spreadsheet open: two months, three bills, and a pattern of excuses that is getting easy to predict.
scene B: {N} has been carrying the gap for weeks. That is a debt, and debts get collected.
scene R: {N} comes home to find the heating off, the flatmate's new trainers in the hall, and their own patience gone.
scene G: The flat felt like a little family once. Now there is a cold silence in the kitchen.
- W | report them to the landlord and let the lease rules deal with it | v: conformity, security | mark: made an enemy | closed: approval: informing on a flatmate; backfire: the whole flat turns against you | chance: 40
- U | keep a precise record of every penny owed, with dates and receipts | v: security | chance: 62
- B | change the wifi password, and hand it over when they pay | v: power | tech: internet | chance: 68
- R | have it out with them in the kitchen, tonight | v: self-direction | chance: 73
- G | cover their share quietly; everyone has a lean month | v: benevolence | mark: helped someone in need | chance: 58
- W>G | start a shared kitty for food and bills, so the flat eats together again | v: benevolence, conformity | chance: 60
- U>W | look up tenancy law and lay out exactly what each person owes | v: universalism (fairness), conformity | chance: 65
- B>U | offer to wipe the debt in exchange for lessons in whatever they do best | v: self-direction, power | mark: learned a skill | chance: 72
- R>B | sell their games console online and keep what they owe | v: power | mark: made an enemy | closed: law: selling what is not yours; backfire: the police, and a counter-claim | tech: internet | chance: 80
- G>R | let it go, and leave when the lease ends, with no hard feelings | v: self-direction | chance: 70
works: The money turns up, with an awkward apology and a pizza.
works: The flat settles into a new arrangement that everyone can live with.
fails: The debt grows, and so does the silence in the kitchen.
fails: The flatmate moves out overnight, leaving the bills and a broken lamp.

== a friend's wedding
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult
age: 18-30
calls: WRG
tone: joy
life: friends, tradition
horizon: week
roles: friend, partner, old school friends, the couple's families
earth: the first of the old school friends to marry: a ceremony, a long dinner and a dance
times: Nearly every young adult, once: the first of the old school friends to marry, mostly in the mid to late twenties (estimate)
once: yes
scene: {friend} is getting married, the first of the old crowd to do it, and {N} is at the back of the hall in their best clothes.
scene W: The vows are old words, said the old way, and {N} finds a lump in their throat as {friend} promises forever.
scene U: {N} watches the ceremony like a visitor watching a rite: who stands where, who cries, what it is all for.
scene B: Half the room are people worth knowing, and the free bar is loosening every tongue.
scene R: The band starts, the first dance ends, and {N} is already pulling people onto the floor.
scene G: Three generations of the family fill the hall, and the grandmother is teaching the young ones the old dance.
- W | give the toast you were asked to give, and give it well | v: benevolence, conformity | mark: kept your word | chance: 90
- U | spend the evening asking the old couples how they knew | v: self-direction | chance: 92
- B | work the room and leave with three useful numbers | habit | v: power, achievement | chance: 78
- R | dance from the first song to the last | v: hedonism | body: light | chance: 95
- G | join the grandmother's circle dance and learn the old steps | v: tradition | mark: made a friend | chance: 87
- W>B | play the perfect guest all day, in front of the people who matter | v: power (face), conformity | chance: 82
- U>R | secretly rehearse the whole table for a surprise dance | v: stimulation, benevolence | chance: 60
- B>G | pay the band for an extra hour so the old folks can keep dancing | v: benevolence, tradition | chance: 85
- R>W | stand up uninvited and make a speech from the heart | v: benevolence | chance: 64
- G>U | sit with the old aunt who knows every family story, and listen | door | v: tradition, self-direction | chance: 86
works: It is the best party in years, and {N} dances with half the room.
works: At the end of the night {friend} hugs {N} and says the day would not have been the same without them.
fails: {N} drinks too much at the reception and says something that cannot be taken back.
fails: The day passes in a blur of small talk, and {N} goes home feeling oddly alone.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- pair 3: calls BR (cycle s1) / calls WUG (cycle s3)
# Both moments are gated alike (excludes: partner), so people with a partner lose the pair whole and stay even.

== a dating app match
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult
age: 18-30
calls: BR
tone: joy
life: love
horizon: week
roles: prospect, friend
excludes: partner
earth: a match on a dating app, and the messages are funny and fast
times: About half of young adults, a few times a year while single and on the apps; 53 in 100 US adults under 30 have used one (Pew 2023)
gap: 0-.5
share: .5
rate: 1
# rate lowered (drafts/repeats/everyday.py)
scene: {N} has matched with {prospect}, and the messages have been flying back and forth since lunch.
scene W: {prospect} seems kind and steady, and asks good questions. {N} wants to do this properly.
scene U: {N} reads the profile of {prospect} three times, looking for what the photos are not saying.
scene B: By the usual standards {prospect} is out of {Ns} league. {N} intends to change the standards.
scene R: {prospect} is funny and gorgeous and suggests a drink tonight, and {Ns} heart is already racing.
scene G: Something about {prospect} feels familiar, like someone {N} has always known.
- W | suggest a proper first date: dinner on Saturday, the bill split evenly | v: conformity, universalism (fairness) | chance: 62
- U | look them up online before agreeing to meet | v: security | chance: 80
- B | keep three other matches going, just in case | habit | v: power, hedonism | chance: 65
- R | say yes to a drink tonight, right now | door | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 72
- G | ask to meet on a long walk by the river, and trust your gut | v: self-direction | chance: 62
- W>U | ask for a photo of their ID before you agree to meet | v: security | act: ask for a photo of the match's ID before agreeing to meet | closed: approval: asking a date for proof of identity; backfire: they take offence and stop replying | chance: 50
- U>B | study which profiles get replies, and rewrite your own to win | v: achievement, power | chance: 68
- B>R | fib about your job to make the chat more exciting | v: stimulation | chance: 45
- R>G | say straight out that you want something real, not a game | v: benevolence, security | chance: 65
- G>W | bring them along to your friends' barbecue for a first date | v: security, benevolence | chance: 48
works: The first date runs three hours over, and neither of them wants it to end.
works: {prospect} turns out to be exactly who they seemed, and that is a lovely surprise.
fails: {prospect} stops replying halfway through a conversation and never starts again.
fails: The date is a polite, awkward hour, and both of them know it by the second drink.

== learning to cook for yourself
tier: everyday
stages: young_adult
age: 17-30
calls: WUG
tone: joy
life: home, food
horizon: months
roles: grandparent, parent, friend, flatmates
excludes: partner
earth: a first kitchen of your own, and a diet of instant noodles that has to end
times: Nearly every young adult, once, in the first flat or room of their own (estimate)
once: yes
scene: {N} has a kitchen of their own for the first time, a single pan, and no idea what to do with an onion.
scene W: {N} wants to eat properly, the way {parent} always insisted: three meals a day, vegetables included.
scene U: {N} has noticed that cooking is chemistry with better smells, and has bought a thermometer.
scene B: Takeaways are eating {Ns} wages. Cooking is cheaper, and {N} has started to wonder who might be impressed by it.
scene R: {N} stands in the market with no list at all, buying whatever looks wonderful.
scene G: {grandparent} used to make a stew every Sunday. {N} can almost smell it, and wishes they had paid more attention.
- W | plan five dinners every Sunday and cook them through the week | habit | v: security, conformity | chance: 80
- U | learn the techniques properly from a book, one dish at a time | v: self-direction, achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 90
- B | master three impressive dishes to cook for dates and guests | v: power (face), achievement | mark: learned a skill | chance: 85
- R | throw everything that smells good into one pan and see | v: stimulation, hedonism | chance: 58
- G | ring home for the family recipes and cook them until they taste right | habit | v: tradition, benevolence | chance: 78
- W>R | host a proper Sunday lunch for friends every week, and let it get loud | habit | v: hedonism, benevolence | chance: 75
- U>G | learn what is in season and cook only that | v: universalism (nature) | chance: 82
- B>W | trade your cooking for the flatmates' cleaning, and hold them to the deal | v: security, power | chance: 68
- R>U | try a wildly hard recipe on a whim, and fail until it works | v: stimulation, achievement | chance: 78
- G>B | grow herbs and tomatoes on the windowsill instead of buying them | v: security, power (resources) | chance: 68
works: The first real dinner comes out of the oven looking just like the picture.
works: {N} cooks for friends, and nobody can tell it was a first attempt.
fails: The smoke alarm goes off, and dinner is toast, again.
fails: The rice welds itself to the pan, and supper is cereal once more.

